  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 310 PM EDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nAfternoon GOES WV imagery indicates a broad troughing pattern across \ncentral and eastern Canada with zonal flow over the Great Lakes. A \nshortwave to the south this morning kept better convective activity \nover southern WI. As the northern flank of this shortwave energy \nmatches up with a cold front reinforced by the lake breeze this \nafternoon, some showers and weak storm development is possible \nwithin the CWA (20-50% chance). Temps have warmed into the upper 60s \nto upper 70s atop Tds in the low to mid 60s, yielding SBCAPE of up \nto around 1000 J/kg over the west and 500 over the east. Low level \nlapse rates have reached up to 7-7.5 C/km in the west, but mid level \nlapse rates are lower at 6.5 C/km. Shear will be strong (~40kts), \nbut strong to severe storms are not expected given the more \ntransient forcing. Isolated showers and storms are anticipated into \nthis evening from west to east before dry weather returns the rest \nof tonight. Probabilities for greater than 0.25 inches of rain are \nonly 10-40%. Some patchy fog is expected tonight, especially where \nshowers occur today. Lows settle into the mid 40s to upper 50s, \ncoldest interior west.\n\nMonday into Monday night remains dry under weak ridging. Highs will \nbe seasonable in the 70s to low 80s under partly sunny skies. Lows \nMonday night will be slightly warmer in the 50s to low 60s.\n\nThe rest of the week sees a slow trend to slightly cooler than \nnormal temps as highs trend toward upper 60s to mid 70s for the end \nof the work week with lows in the 40s to mid 50s. Shortwaves Tuesday \nand Wednesday yield chances for showers and storms (25-50% chance) \nwith plenty of timing/coverage uncertainty. Bulk shear will be \nstrong, but instability is not forecast to be very impressive so \nsevere weather is not expected at this time. Finally the broad \nCanadian troughing pattern breaks down late this week and stronger \nhigh pressure returns to the Great Lakes, favoring drier weather \ninto the weekend. PoPs return late Saturday into Sunday as ensembles \ntrack a low pressure system into the region.",
